{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Cain said to Abel his brother.</b> Meaning: he said to him a word of brotherhood. Because the cause of hatred had already preceded, arising from jealousy, Cain would make Abel sense from the very manner of the matter that he hated him, so that Abel would take care to be on guard against him. Therefore he spoke to him the word of brotherhood — for no hatred had been born in Abel's heart on account of the matter, since Abel was not jealous. And Cain deceived him in order to gain his trust, for the purpose of his intention to bring him down into the pit, as Scripture states after this. And therefore the pauser of verses acted wisely — he made a break [in the text] that is not a break, between this verse and the verse that follows it." "<b>And it came to pass, when they were in the field.</b> One needs to know for what purpose Scripture informed us that they were in the field. Also, why does it say <b>וַיָּקָם</b> — why this rising? Scripture should have needed to say only ״Cain killed Abel.״ Further, it should not have needed to say ״his brother.״ Further, one needs to know the meaning of His saying <b>Where is Abel</b> — what is the Creator's intention in this question? Further, who is as foolish as Cain, that he would answer the One who knows all and is witness ״I do not know״ — and who recognizes His providence like the first created beings? <b>Further,</b> why does He lengthen the expression to say <b>your brother's keeper</b>? Further, His saying ״The voice of your brother's bloods cries out to me from the ground״ — why was it necessary to say <b>from the ground</b>, and what is this cry? Further, why did He curse the ground? If he is the murderer, upon him should the curse come — what does the ground rise or fall in his murder? If the ground does not accept his blood, it will be for the licker to lick, and its actions seem to suit covering his blood, and there is almost no significance to HaShem's words:" "<b>However,</b> the intention is that Cain was jealous of Abel, thinking that he was the cause of his degradation — for through Abel's mediation Cain's darkness was recognized. And he thought that when Abel would no longer exist, HaShem would be reconciled to Cain, for there would be no one else to choose. He thought to kill him, but feared that his stench and foulness would rise and be manifest when he laid hands on him, for he had been commanded against murder. So he devised counsels to kill him without laying a hand on him. Now this man, a worker of the ground, knew its exits and entrances and was expert in its chambers. And they of blessed memory said (Ecclesiastes Rabbah 1:4; Nedarim 39b) that the earth has limbs like the limbs of a man: eyes, hands, and feet, a mouth, etc. — and one who knows it knows the places. From this, the evil counselor found a way to kill him by cunning, without laying his hand on him. And that is what is meant by <b>and it came to pass, when they were in the field</b> — walking about the earth, he found a place for his designs. And through what preceded — that he had deceived him with a show of brotherhood — Abel trusted him, and upon this he rose against him. And in this rising, that he rose — meaning, he stood over him — in this he killed him. Now the standing itself does not kill; rather, he was cunning to stand over him in a place where Abel would be swallowed by the earth, and that was his death — and the earth is what kills. And although Scripture did not specify here the manner of killing, nevertheless He revealed it in the verse that follows this one, where He said ״which opened.״" "And HaShem, who examines hearts, asked him ״Where is Abel your brother,״ to see whether he would confess his crime. He answered ״I do not know.״ And if you would say — you should have paid attention to him — therefore He said <b>Are you your brother's keeper?</b> and the rest. That is: did you make me a keeper over him? And he did not confess his evil deed, for he thought that HaShem would not punish for the cause, or would not take note of it. HaShem answered him ״What have you done?״ — He informed him that his evil deeds are the cause for the earth to swallow Abel. And that is what is said: <b>The voice of your brother's bloods cries out to me from the ground</b> — meaning that Abel thinks in his mind that the earth killed him, and he complains before HaShem against it. And therefore HaShem cursed him, for he is the cause. And together with his curse, He also cursed the earth that opened its mouth to receive Abel from his hand — that it should not open its mouth again. And this is what they of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 37b): it did not open its mouth again, and it sings song and music to El Elyon — a obligation required to be performed at its edges, as it is written (Isaiah 24:16): ״מִכְּנַף הָאָרֶץ זְמִרוֹת שָׁמַעְנוּ.״ And it trembled at the time HaShem told it to swallow the Egyptians — because of the dread of its opening its mouth to swallow Abel — until HaShem swore to it (Mekhilta), as it is written (Exodus 15:12): ״נָטִיתָ יְמִינְךָ״, and He swore to it, and then it swallowed them in its mouth. And it is possible that this is what they of blessed memory intended (Genesis Rabbah 22), and this is their language: the Rabbis said: he killed him with a stone — end of quote. Now with this manner of killing, his entire body would become wound upon wound, and that is why it says ״דְּמֵי״ in the plural — for his death was not from one place, in the manner of slaughtered animals. And through this Abel was stripped of every evil cleaving, as I explained in the verse ״I have acquired a man with HaShem.״ And there are many midrashim in the verse."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:34.122128139Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}